---
description: An online worker with a ready model must keep receiving tasks
alwaysApply: true
---

# Worker task delivery

A phone that is online, available, and has Gemma ready must keep asking for tasks. Heartbeats alone are not task delivery.

## Server

- Completing a task must remove that phone from `_in_flight_devices`. `mark_completed` has to free the phone. Leaving it "running" makes every later `claim_next_assignment` return nothing for that device.
- One finished task must not block the next queued task for the same phone.
- Cover this with a regression test: claim, `mark_completed`, enqueue another task, claim again and get the new task.

## Phone

- The assignment loop must survive a rejected poll. A low battery, a constraint exception, or a failed request must skip that attempt and keep looping. Do not let the exception end `_runAutoAssignmentLoop` while the heartbeat timer keeps running.
- Below `ExecutionPolicy.minimumBatteryPercent` (20), do not take work, but resume polling when the battery is high enough again. Do not require the user to force-close the app.
- `canAcceptAssignments` stays false until Gemma is actually ready. A downloaded file that failed activation is not ready, and the UI must not present it as ready.
- When the phone radio has no internet, show the offline message as an overlay. Do not push or remove a navigator route for it, and do not start HTTP, heartbeat, or foreground-service work until the radio is back.
- The execution foreground service must not crash the process. Do not call `startForegroundService` again while it is already running, and do not start it only to stop it. On Android 14+ keep the long-running watch service on `specialUse` (dataSync is time-limited and the system Close app dialog appears when that limit or the start deadline is missed). If Android rejects or times out the service, stop it and start it again when the app is resumed.
